{"step_index":455,"source":"SYSTEM","type":"CHECKPOINT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-29T13:54:44Z","content":"# Resuming from a compaction\n\nYou are continuing work on the task described above, but you have lost access to the full conversation history, and need to resume work efficiently using the progress summary below:\n\n# User Requests\nThe following were user requests from the truncated conversation in chronological order:\n1. MASTER BUILD PROMPT — FRAUDLENS AI\n\nZeckathon PS2: Explainable Financial Risk & Fraud Analytics\n\nAct as a senior AI/ML engineer, full-stack developer, financial fraud analyst, cybersecurity engineer, product designer, and hackathon-winning technical mentor.\n\nBuild a complete, runnable, innovative prototype named FraudLens AI.\n\nTagline: Detect smarter. Explain every alert. Reduce false alarms.\n\nCore mission: Predict potentially fraudulent transactions, identify suspicious relationships among transactions, explain why each transaction was flagged, identify uncertain cases, and help investigators decide what to inspect next.\n\nDo not merely create a dashboard with static numbers. Build working data processing, a trained machine-learning pipeline, API endpoints, interactive visualizations, and a usable investigator workflow.\n\nDo not claim that the project is globally unprecedented. Create a distinctive implementation combining transaction prediction, relationship analysis, chronological replay, uncertainty-aware review, and evidence-grounded investigation.\n\n---\n\n1. REQUIRED TECHNOLOGY STACK\n\nUse a maintainable architecture:\n\n- Frontend: React, TypeScript, Vite, Tailwind CSS.\n- Visualization: Plotly or Recharts for charts; React Flow or Cytoscape.js for transaction relationship graphs.\n- Backend: Python FastAPI.\n- Data processing: Pandas and NumPy.\n- Machine learning: scikit-learn; compare Logistic Regression and Random Forest or HistGradientBoosting as suitable.\n- Explainability: SHAP where compatible and computationally practical; otherwise implement transparent feature contributions and clearly identify their limitations.\n- Graph analysis: NetworkX for connected components, shared-entity analysis, and relationship metrics.\n- Database: SQLite for local demo; use a repository abstraction that can later support PostgreSQL.\n- Testing: pytest for backend and prediction tests; appropriate frontend checks.\n- API documentation: FastAPI's generated Swagger UI.\n\nCreate a clean project structure with separate frontend, backend, model, data, tests, and documentation folders.\n\nPin compatible dependencies. Provide a \".env.example\", \".gitignore\", sample dataset, and clear README.\n\nThe project must run locally on a normal student laptop without paid APIs, bank credentials, or mandatory cloud services.\n\n---\n\n2. BUILD A REAL PREDICTION ENGINE\n\nImplement a genuine machine-learning classification pipeline, not random scores or hardcoded predictions.\n\nSupport features available in the selected dataset, such as:\n\n- Transaction amount and amount deviation from historical behavior.\n- Transaction frequency in configurable time windows.\n- Account age and account activity.\n- Device novelty and device reuse.\n- IP/location mismatch, when valid fields exist.\n- Merchant category and merchant risk history, when available.\n- Failed attempts, where represented in the data.\n- Connections to other accounts or transactions through shared entities.\n\nDo not invent unavailable features. Handle missing columns explicitly and document any unavailable signals.\n\nTraining pipeline\n\n1. Load a documented public fraud dataset or a clearly labelled synthetic dataset.\n2. Validate column types, missing values, duplicates, and label definitions.\n3. Separate training and evaluation data correctly.\n4. Avoid data leakage, particularly using future transactions or target-derived features.\n5. Account for severe class imbalance through appropriate class weighting or training-only resampling.\n6. Compare a simple baseline with the selected model.\n7. Tune the review threshold using validation data, not the final test set.\n8. Save the fitted preprocessing pipeline and model.\n9. Load the saved model for subsequent predictions instead of retraining for every transaction.\n10. Include model version, feature schema, and training dataset information.\n\nEvaluate with precision, recall, F1, PR-AUC when appropriate, false-positive rate, confusion matrix, and precision at a limited review capacity.\n\nNever fabricate performance metrics. Clearly label results as measured, simulated, or unavailable.\n\nIf using synthetic training data, state that the model has not been validated on real banking traffic.\n\n---\n\n3. CREATE A THREE-OUTCOME TRIAGE SYSTEM\n\nEvery prediction should produce:\n\nLOWER RISK\n\nThe model detects fewer concerning signals. Display the score and retain appropriate monitoring.\n\nHIGH RISK\n\nThe transaction warrants investigation based on its estimated risk and the chosen review policy.\n\nUNCERTAIN\n\nThe evidence is ambiguous, the model is poorly calibrated, the prediction is near a decision boundary, or important information is missing.\n\nDo not equate uncertainty with fraud.\n\nCalculate and display separately:\n\n- Model fraud score.\n- Probability estimate, only if properly calibrated.\n- Uncertainty indicator, with its calculation documented.\n- Operational review priority.\n- Final investigator decision.\n\nDo not display an arbitrary number as a mathematically valid fraud probability. Explain how every score is calculated.\n\nMake thresholds configurable and show how changing a threshold affects review volume, precision, and recall on the evaluation set.\n\nNo model should automatically accuse a customer of fraud. Treat results as risk assessments, not proof of wrongdoing.\n\n---\n\n4. DISTINCTIVE FEATURE: FRAUDLENS REPLAY LAB\n\nMake this a major part of the prototype.\n\nCreate an interactive transaction replay environment that allows a judge to select a transaction and investigate the surrounding activity.\n\nA. Transaction timeline\n\nShow earlier and later events with timestamps, amounts, device identifiers, merchant identifiers, and available location signals.\n\nClearly distinguish historical information available at prediction time from later information that would create data leakage.\n\nB. Behavioral change detector\n\nCompare the selected event with a documented baseline of previous behavior.\n\nHighlight changes such as:\n\n- An unusually large amount relative to prior activity.\n- Several transactions within a short time.\n- A newly observed device.\n- An unusual merchant category.\n- A newly observed connection to other accounts.\n\nUse neutral language such as “unusual compared with observed history.” Do not assert that unusual activity is necessarily criminal.\n\nC. Relationship graph\n\nCreate an interactive graph connecting transactions, accounts, merchants, devices, and IP addresses when the dataset contains those fields.\n\nHighlight:\n\n- Shared devices.\n- Reused IP addresses.\n- Rapid transaction bursts.\n- Connected suspicious transactions.\n- Potential clusters requiring investigation.\n\nDisplay why each edge exists and the underlying evidence.\n\nDistinguish an observed relationship from an inferred risk. A shared device or IP address alone is not proof of fraud.\n\nDo not invent connections between unrelated records.\n\nD. Counterfactual simulator\n\nAllow the investigator to change one or more input signals and recalculate the model output.\n\nExample:\n“What if this transaction amount were closer to the account's historical range?”\n\nDisplay the original score, modified score, changed inputs, and model version.\n\nWarn that changing one feature while keeping everything else fixed may produce an unrealistic scenario. Do not describe a counterfactual as a causal explanation or a guaranteed way to avoid fraud detection.\n\nE. Evidence-grounded case summary\n\nGenerate a concise investigation summary using only actual model outputs and available transaction records.\n\nInclude:\n\n- Observed evidence.\n- Main score contributors.\n- Related records.\n- Missing information.\n- Uncertainty.\n- Recommended verification steps.\n\nDo not use a language model to invent transaction facts. If a generative model is unavailable, use deterministic templates.\n\n---\n\n5. BUILD A REVIEW QUEUE THAT OPTIMIZES INVESTIGATOR TIME\n\nCreate a sortable, searchable investigation queue.\n\nColumns:\n\n- Transaction ID.\n- Timestamp.\n- Amount.\n- Risk score.\n- Risk category.\n- Uncertainty indicator.\n- Main reason codes.\n- Related transaction count.\n- Investigation status.\n- Assigned investigator.\n\nSupport filters for risk level, uncertainty, time, merchant, amount, and status.\n\nCreate a documented review-priority calculation that considers estimated fraud risk, uncertainty, potential loss where justified, and review capacity.\n\nAvoid treating a large transaction as automatically more suspicious. Keep the risk score distinct from potential financial impact.\n\nShow why each transaction was placed at its position in the queue.\n\nProvide a queue simulation in which the user chooses a daily review capacity and sees the number of cases selected. Evaluate top-ranked cases using actual test labels where available.\n\n---\n\n6. BUILD INVESTIGATOR FEEDBACK AND AUDIT HISTORY\n\nWhen an investigator opens a case, allow them to record:\n\n- Confirmed fraud.\n- Legitimate transaction.\n- Inconclusive.\n- Escalated for further checks.\n\nCapture the reviewer decision, timestamp, optional notes, and model version.\n\nStore feedback in SQLite with appropriate input validation.\n\nShow a history of previous decisions and overrides.\n\nDo not silently retrain the model on every click. Create a controlled workflow for reviewing labels, checking data quality, retraining, comparing model versions, and approving a new model.\n\nClearly distinguish an investigator's preliminary opinion from independently confirmed fraud.\n\n---\n\n7. DESIGN THE USER INTERFACE\n\nBuild a polished, premium financial intelligence interface.\n\nVisual direction:\n\n- Deep navy background.\n- Slate surfaces.\n- Restrained cyan and teal accents.\n- Amber for review.\n- Red for high-risk warnings.\n- High-contrast readable typography.\n- Clear spacing, consistent cards, responsive layouts.\n- Subtle animation only where it improves comprehension.\n\nAvoid excessive neon effects, excessive gradients, oversized headings, meaningless decorative charts, fake customer logos, and generic AI landing-page templates.\n\nCreate these screens:\n\n1. Overview dashboard.\n2. Transaction investigation queue.\n3. Individual transaction detail.\n4. Fraud Network Radar.\n5. FraudLens Replay Lab.\n6. Model evaluation and confusion matrix.\n7. Investigator feedback history.\n8. Dataset upload and \n<truncated 5522 bytes>\n2. continue\n\n<summary>\n\n## 1. Task Overview\n\n**Core Request:** The user said \"Continue\" — this is a continuation of a prior session building **FraudLens AI**, a full-stack explainable financial fraud detection application for **Zeckathon PS2** hackathon. The project combines ML-based fraud scoring, NetworkX graph analysis, a FastAPI backend, React/TypeScript frontend, and SQLite persistence.\n\n**Project Location:** `C:\\Users\\S Nandhakumar\\.gemini\\antigravity\\scratch\\fraudlens-ai`\n\n**Success Criteria:** A complete, working full-stack application with:\n- Synthetic dataset generation with documented fraud typologies\n- ML training pipeline with zero data leakage (chronological split)\n- Three-outcome triage (HIGH RISK / UNCERTAIN / LOWER RISK)\n- Transparent feature contributions / reason codes\n- NetworkX graph-based syndicate detection\n- FraudLens Replay Lab (timeline replay, behavioral drift, counterfactual what-if)\n- Investigation queue with priority ranking\n- Investigator feedback/audit trail with override tracking\n- CSV batch upload with validation\n- Real-time simulation mode\n- React SPA served from FastAPI\n- Automated pytest test suite (11 tests all passing)\n\n## 2. Progress\n\n### Fully Completed & Verified:\n\n**Backend (Python/FastAPI):**\n- [x] `backend/app/ml/data_generator.py` — Synthetic dataset generator (2479 transactions, ~3.15% fraud, 4 fraud typologies + 3 golden demo cases with pre-seeded history for ACC-DEMO-01)\n- [x] `backend/app/ml/features.py` — Zero-leakage FeatureExtractor (11 features, chronological state machine)\n- [x] `backend/app/ml/predictor.py` — FraudLensPredictor class (risk scoring, 3-outcome triage, uncertainty, reason codes). **Thresholds: tau_low=0.20, tau_high=0.65 (defaults)**\n- [x] `backend/app/ml/train.py` — Training pipeline (HistGradientBoosting champion + LogisticRegression baseline, validation threshold tuning). **Tuned thresholds: tau_low=0.20, tau_high=0.80** (validation F1=1.0, test F1=1.0, ROC-AUC=1.0, Precision=100%, Recall=100%)\n- [x] `backend/app/graph/engine.py` — NetworkX FraudGraphEngine (ego graph, syndicate cluster detection)\n- [x] `backend/app/db/repository.py` — SQLite DatabaseManager, TransactionRepository, FeedbackRepository\n- [x] `backend/app/db/init_data.py` — Database seeder (scores all transactions, seeds 2 initial feedback records)\n- [x] `backend/app/api/endpoints.py` — 17 REST API endpoints (health, overview, queue, detail, predict, counterfactual, timeline, behavioral-change, graph ego/syndicates, feedback CRUD, queue simulation, demo cases, reset, CSV upload, simulation event)\n- [x] `backend/app/schemas/transactions.py` — Pydantic request/response models\n- [x] `backend/app/main.py` — FastAPI app with CORS, static file serving for React SPA\n- [x] `backend/tests/test_api_and_ml.py` — 11 automated tests (**ALL 11 PASSING**)\n\n**Data & Model Artifacts:**\n- [x] `data/synthetic_transactions.csv` — 2479 records generated\n- [x] `data/dataset_metadata.json` — Dataset metadata\n- [x] `data/fraudlen